{"title":"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 24","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(Quick,_Not_rigorous)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (Quick, Not rigorous)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the perimeter of the boundary of the region consisting of all points which can be expressed as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/4/72490f2256ee7aaeaee1d84f6a453b311af15612.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(2u-3w, v+4w)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/e/e/3eec5c742c766f9785f67dd009b66cbb3295f4b5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le u\\le1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"79\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/2/9a25be27db6105d4dd738dfe596d43bf3bbf1233.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le v\\le1,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"83\" height=\"17\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/0/3a0237f66ce6139de8adffe57bb1e5d04312e4af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le w\\le1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"82\" height=\"16\" />?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/0/2c07d024335e20a2e5a2cf07daf2dbe1deecf0eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 10\\sqrt{3} \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 13 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) } 12 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 18 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"425\" height=\"23\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/5/af574073c4835f41cd61d97367eea56cf2a89330.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] import geometry; pair A = (-3, 4); pair B = (-3, 5); pair C = (-1, 4); pair D = (-1, 5);   pair AA = (0, 0); pair BB = (0, 1); pair CC = (2, 0); pair DD = (2, 1);    //draw(A--B--D--C--cycle);    draw(A--B); label(&quot;1&quot;,midpoint(A--B),W); label(&quot;2&quot;,midpoint(D--B),N); draw(A--C,dashed); draw(B--D); draw(C--D, dashed);  draw(A--AA); label(&quot;5&quot;,midpoint(A--AA),W); draw(B--BB,dashed); draw(C--CC,dashed); draw(D--DD); label(&quot;5&quot;,midpoint(D--DD),E); label(&quot;1&quot;,midpoint(CC--DD),E); label(&quot;2&quot;,midpoint(AA--CC),S);  // Dotted vertices dot(A); dot(B); dot(C); dot(D);    dot(AA); dot(BB); dot(CC); dot(DD);  draw(AA--BB,dashed); draw(AA--CC); draw(BB--DD,dashed); draw(CC--DD);  label(&quot;(0,0)&quot;,AA,W); label(&quot;(-3,4)&quot;,A,SW); label(&quot;(-1,5)&quot;,D,E); label(&quot;(2,1)&quot;,DD,NE); [/asy]\" width=\"252\" height=\"195\" />\n\tNotice that we are given a parametric form of the region, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is used in both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />. We first fix <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and graph <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/d/5adfa23a44a8a8920ab3855720196480c6de41ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-3w,4w)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"83\" height=\"20\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/0/3a0237f66ce6139de8adffe57bb1e5d04312e4af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0\\le w\\le1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"82\" height=\"16\" />. When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we have the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, and when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, we have the point <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b69fae7e4c2a4549fe5013d40768e9ef2aa07d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-3,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" />. We see that since this is a directly proportional function, we can just connect the dots like this: \n</p><p>\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/9/1a976e150369fa1da5084c6f484568fcf12f96cb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] \timport graph; \tLabel f; size(5cm); \tunitsize(0.7cm);  \txaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); \tyaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  \tdraw((0,0)--(-3,4)); \t[/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>\tNow, when we vary <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/9/339d208c973e94087dc849c1a3efd8a13d5ca98f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, this line is translated to the right <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> units: \n</p><p>\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/6/f063272dfdc63985b97134cfde1f7085f54fd334.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] \timport graph; \tLabel f;  \tunitsize(0.7cm); size(5cm); \txaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); \tyaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  \tdraw((0,0)--(-3,4)); \tdraw((2,0)--(-1,4)); \t[/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>\tWe know that any points in the region between the line (or rather segment) and its translation satisfy <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, so we shade in the region: \n</p><p>\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/0/0900b601fa269b3d8e583fb177bee3e5b937d1dc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] \timport graph; \tLabel f;  \tunitsize(0.7cm); size(5cm); \txaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); \tyaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  \tdraw((0,0)--(-3,4)); \tdraw((2,0)--(-1,4));  \tfilldraw((0,0)--(-3,4)--(-1,4)--(2,0)--cycle, gray); \t[/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>\tWe can also shift this quadrilateral one unit up, because of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. Thus, this is our figure: \n</p><p>\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/f/b0f90cbef8a2438f80c91b347b7569f5a3001f82.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] \timport graph; \tLabel f;  \tunitsize(0.7cm); size(5cm); \txaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); \tyaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  \tdraw((0,0)--(-3,4)); \tdraw((2,0)--(-1,4));  \tfilldraw((0,0)--(-3,4)--(-1,4)--(2,0)--cycle, gray); \tfilldraw((0,1)--(-3,5)--(-1,5)--(2,1)--cycle, gray);  draw((0,0)--(0,1),black+dashed); draw((2,0)--(2,1),black+dashed); draw((-3,4)--(-3,5),black+dashed); \t[/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>\t<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/0/f808e73f7b6ffb1a1ee30cd79de5b88afe524df9.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] \timport graph; \tLabel f;  \tunitsize(0.7cm); size(5cm); \txaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); \tyaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  \tdraw((0,0)--(-3,4)); \tdraw((1,0)--(-2,4));  \tfilldraw((0,0)--(2,0)--(2,1)--(-1,5)--(-3,5)--(-3,4)--cycle, gray); \t[/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>\tThe length of the boundary is simply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/c/0accd0286c34abbc5da842909827e37a571a2a96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1+2+5+1+2+5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"167\" height=\"15\" /> (<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> can be obtained by Pythagorean theorem since we have side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/c/c7cab1a05e1e0c1d51a6a219d96577a16b7abf9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.). This equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/a/a/eaa8bfe35193765ebd86513f34a12f43ce3d92fb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E) }16.}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"70\" height=\"30\" /> \n</p><p>~Technodoggo ~ESAOPS\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can find the \"boundary points\" and work with our intuition to solve the problem. We set each of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/f/64f3d9f1bd93f9e55f2970a79627f9ebbf7080a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u, v, w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" /> equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/c/dbce1918021fbaf32f63344dd40acb93cfb5260f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0, 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"28\" height=\"17\" /> for a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/5/8455f3b5cb3b4880b8c9d782a5c1f0334db819eb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> combinations in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/f/64f3d9f1bd93f9e55f2970a79627f9ebbf7080a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u, v, w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" />. We now test each one.\n</p><p>Case 1: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/8/4/984411f728046d88807e50d4a7a351302bcee8c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 0, v = 0, w = 0 \\implies (0, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" />  \n</p><p>Case 2: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/a/45af8a2a866f48df0c84094ac731731c7928a177.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 0, v = 0, w = 1 \\implies (-3, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"255\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Case 3: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/e/17ee75a8b73a089a404ffeb2ea62e36e93cdc68e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 0, v = 1, w = 0 \\implies (0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Case 4: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/1/89175a930dbcbd4cd404fed7a02af8d1edd772e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 0, v = 1, w = 1 \\implies (-3, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"255\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Case 5: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/3/9239988f2b2a6b34b69bc1474a2f91237a0d41f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 1, v = 0, w = 0 \\implies (2, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Case 6: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/d/9dd4c6bcce7e2f5490a1cb752a1e881df36c451b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 1, v = 0, w = 1 \\implies (-1, 4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"255\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Case 7: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/8/a483490ad2c08d7f92f1a7d4c0434ad75d60557d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 1, v = 1, w = 0 \\implies (2, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"241\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>Case 8: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/3/243d407b1afcca3f46886de4b933788cd2094118.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u = 1, v = 1, w = 1 \\implies (-1, 5)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"255\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p>When graphed on a coordinate plane, the points appear as follows.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/0/7/507a2dbc1108bbbc30133a6e6c9ce8e000ac307d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  import graph; import geometry; Label f;  size(5cm); unitsize(0.7cm);  xaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); yaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  pair A = (0, 0); dot (A); pair B = (-3, 4); dot (B); pair C = (0, 1); dot (C); pair D = (-3, 5); dot (D);  pair E = (2, 0); dot (E); pair F = (-1, 4); dot (F); pair G = (2, 1); dot (G); pair H = (-1, 5); dot (H);  [/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>Notice how there are two distinct rectangles visible in the figure. This leads us to believe that the region tracks the motion of this region as it travels in space. To understand why this is true, we can imagine a fixed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> (as it is present in both the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> coordinates). Then if we hold one of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> fixed and let the other vary, we get a straight line parallel to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> axis respectively. If we let the other vary, we get the other type of straight line. Together, they form a rectangular region. In addition, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> serves as a diagonal translation, so if we now let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> vary, it traces out the motion of the rectangle. Keeping this in mind, we connect the dots. \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/b/a/dba940b36e4401e657a620ebc63ba994db36dce3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy]  import graph; import geometry; Label f;  size(5cm); unitsize(0.7cm);  xaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0)); yaxis(-5,5,Ticks(f, 5.0, 1.0));  draw((0,0)--(-3,4)--(-3,5)--(-1,5)--(2,1)--(2,0)--cycle, gray);  [/asy]\" width=\"245\" height=\"245\" />\n</p><p>Each of the diagonal sides have length <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> by the distance formula on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/6/f9603ca3089464e548fc6f1366bc474e7efef8d9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0,0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b69fae7e4c2a4549fe5013d40768e9ef2aa07d1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-3,4)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" /> (the other diagonal side is congruent), so our total perimeter is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bd74ba7c3093ad1e4739f22c2bb71882d988476.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2 + 1 + 5 + 2 + 1 + 5 = \\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~ 16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"254\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/User:Cxsmi\">cxsmi</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28Quick.2C_Not_rigorous.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(Quick,_Not_rigorous)\">Solution 3 (Quick, Not rigorous)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (Quick, Not rigorous)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let's assume that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/2/562b2432069493d7adcd754895d48b8cefbb45df.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"49\" height=\"14\" />. If we plug <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/a/e7a520d26d377d503b4ba713785a6493a96cdea2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, 0), (0, 1), (1, 1), (1, 0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"188\" height=\"20\" /> into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/d/9ad99798ec4c38e165cf517cb9e02b1c9e824103.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$u$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/f/a9f23bf124b6b2b2a993eb313c72e678664ac74a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> respectively, we will form a rectangle that has side lengths <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. If we change <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9ee4b825a2e36ae093ed7be5e4851ef453b34914.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"10\" /> to the maximum value (which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />), we will find out that w shifts the rectangle by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/b/2fb4a4864ba62471220eb1b80f3f4deb066c256e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$-3w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"39\" height=\"15\" /> on the x-axis and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/1/a/91ac6a4d678d479ce969c0905a68d04e19712c58.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4w$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> on the y-axis. Since we need to find the perimeter of the shift and rectangle, we can calculate that the length that the corner has move during the shift using the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php/Pythagorean_Theorem?srsltid=AfmBOort48ZEr_91TCSYH_SXPuY9On1yzJxPuh5gqQ8d0P1dp_c8onDg\">Pythagorean Theorem</a>. Thus, the distance is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Now, we can find the perimeter of the boundary which is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/a/38aea96e0c43acf8f2f5a18393ed49a04d67808a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2(1 + 2 + 5) = 16$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"139\" height=\"20\" />. This means that the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/f/1/1f192ba0b66748f21b3a6b5a26d4138eb9146d60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(E)}~ 16}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"64\" height=\"30\" />.\n</p><p>~ROGER8432V3\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_1_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution 1 by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/KEV3ka5gWYU\">https://youtu.be/KEV3ka5gWYU</a>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution\">Video Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/bqIlsWTOL3k\">https://youtu.be/bqIlsWTOL3k</a>\n</p><p>~Steven Chen (Professor Chen Education Palace, www.professorchenedu.com)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_24&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B\">2023 AMC 10B</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Answer_Key\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c13\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">Problem 23</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25\">Problem 25</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_1\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_2\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_3\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_4\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 6\">6</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 7\">7</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_8\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 8\">8</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_9\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 9\">9</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_10\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 10\">10</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_11\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 11\">11</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_12\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 12\">12</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_13\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 13\">13</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_14\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 14\">14</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_15\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 15\">15</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_16\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 16\">16</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_17\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 17\">17</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_18\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 18\">18</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_19\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 19\">19</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 20\">20</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_21\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 21\">21</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 22\">22</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23\">23</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">24</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2023_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_25\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"2023 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 25\">25</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=AMC_10_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"AMC 10 Problems and Solutions\"> All AMC 10 Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<div class=\"print\">\n<div style=\"display:flex;width:100%;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center\">\n<div>\n<p><span class=\"mw-default-size\" typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:AMC_Logo.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/d/d1/AMC_Logo.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"97\" height=\"84\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"flex-grow:1;text-align:right\">\n<p>These problems are copyrighted &#169; by the <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://www.maa.org\">Mathematical Association of America</a>. \n</p>\n</div>\n<div style=\"clear:both;\"></div>\n</div></div>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017172926\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.052 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.071 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 492/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 2324/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 158/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 16291/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   13.881      1 -total\n 58.18%    8.076      1 Template:AMC10_box\n 40.92%    5.680      1 Template:MAA_Notice\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:25715:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017172926 and revision id 261725. Rendering was triggered because: api-parse\n -->\n</div>"}